---
const chapters = [
  {id:'change-summary',label:'Executive summary'},
  {id:'change-problem',label:'The problem'},
  {id:'change-role',label:'Role & leadership'},
  {id:'change-horizons',label:'Two horizons'},
  {id:'change-fix',label:'Immediate fix'},
  {id:'change-north-star',label:'North star'},
  {id:'change-system',label:'Workflow'},
  {id:'change-people',label:'Kit & Nik'},
  {id:'change-outcome',label:'Outcome'},
  {id:'change-reflection',label:'Reflection'},
];

const systemMoves = [
  ['01','Ticket ownership','Link every change to a durable ticket with a named owner, purpose and local control.'],
  ['02','Review workflow','Give reviewers a queue, submitted-change context and a clear inspect-before-approval path.'],
  ['03','Stage and release','Show when approved changes are ready to move forward instead of hiding the last-mile handoff.'],
  ['04','Traceability','Keep comments, approvals, decisions and change logs attached to the work across handoffs.'],
  ['05','Role-aware experience','Give Kit and Nik the context their responsibilities require without making either learn the entire permission model.'],
  ['06','Phased evolution','Ship the immediate safety layer, then use the north star to guide a fuller collaboration system.'],
];
---

<div class="deep-change" data-change-case>
  <nav class="change-chapter-nav" aria-label="Change Management chapters">
    <p>Inside this case</p>
    <ol>{chapters.map(chapter=><li><a href={`#${chapter.id}`}>{chapter.label}</a></li>)}</ol>
  </nav>

  <details class="change-chapter-menu">
    <summary>Explore this case study <span aria-hidden="true">＋</span></summary>
    <nav aria-label="Change Management chapters on mobile"><ol>{chapters.map(chapter=><li><a href={`#${chapter.id}`}>{chapter.label}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>
  </details>

  <main class="change-narrative">
    <section id="change-summary" class="change-chapter change-lead">
      <p class="change-index">01 / Executive summary</p>
      <h2>Policy collisions were a <em>coordination-confidence</em> problem.</h2>
      <div class="lead-grid"><p>Several administrators could edit the same firewall policy across teams, roles and time zones. The product treated each edit as a configuration action, while the organisation experienced it as shared work with ownership, consequence and risk.</p><p class="lead-note">Over eight months, the design reduced immediate collision risk while shaping a more complete collaboration strategy—because a lock was useful, but it was never the whole answer.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section id="change-problem" class="change-chapter problem-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">02 / The problem</p>
      <h2>Correct policy. Missing coordination.</h2>
      <p class="problem-summary">One administrator could overwrite another, ownership could become ambiguous, and urgent edits could bypass the context needed for a safe review. The interface showed configuration state—but not the social state surrounding it.</p>
      <ul class="tension-list"><li><span>Visibility</span>Know who is actively working on a policy before making another change.</li><li><span>Collision prevention</span>Stop parallel edits from silently overwriting one another.</li><li><span>Reviewability</span>Give approvers the intent and evidence needed to judge a change.</li><li><span>Traceability</span>Preserve ownership and decisions when work moves between people.</li></ul>
      <aside class="editorial-note">A firewall can reject bad traffic. It is less talented at resolving unclear team ownership.</aside>
    </section>

    <section id="change-role" class="change-chapter dark-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">03 / Role & leadership</p>
      <h2>I connected product urgency with research, architecture and execution.</h2>
      <div class="role-grid"><p>I led the design end to end, but more importantly created the structure that helped the team respond well under pressure. I framed the immediate fix, shaped the longer-term north star, and translated cross-functional input into a coherent, safer change-management story.</p><dl><div><dt>Owned</dt><dd>Problem framing, workflow model and end-to-end interaction design</dd></div><div><dt>Core partnership</dt><dd>Product manager, UX researcher and engineering architect</dd></div><div><dt>Leadership move</dt><dd>Separated immediate risk reduction from system-level transformation</dd></div><div><dt>Delivery approach</dt><dd>Ship a bounded fix, then align the organisation around the north star</dd></div></dl></div>
    </section>

    <section id="change-horizons" class="change-chapter horizons-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">04 / Two horizons</p>
      <h2>One problem. Two clocks.</h2>
      <p class="measure">The current release needed a practical safeguard. The product needed a better collaboration model. Treating those as the same deadline would have produced either a late fix or a shallow vision.</p>
      <div class="horizon-grid"><article><span>Horizon 01 · Now</span><h3>Prevent accidental collisions.</h3><p>Introduce visible policy locking and read-only protection as the immediate safety layer.</p><strong>Measure of success</strong><small>Administrators can see ownership and avoid overwriting active work.</small></article><article><span>Horizon 02 · Next</span><h3>Make change collaboration legible.</h3><p>Build a system for authoring, review, staging, deployment and traceable handoffs across roles.</p><strong>Measure of success</strong><small>Teams understand who owns the change, what happens next and why it is safe.</small></article></div>
    </section>

    <section id="change-fix" class="change-chapter fix-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">05 / Immediate fix</p>
      <h2>The first move was not perfection. It was reducing collision risk inside a brutal delivery window.</h2>
      <div class="fix-sequence">
        <article>
          <span>01</span>
          <div class="fix-step-copy"><strong>Claim temporary ownership</strong><p>An administrator can deliberately lock the policy before editing, making ownership explicit rather than implied.</p></div>
          <figure><a href="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-01.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open the claim temporary ownership interface at full size"><img src="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-01.png" alt="Firepower Management Center policy interface with the control used to lock or unlock a policy highlighted" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>Start with an explicit lock—not a silent assumption.</figcaption></figure>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>02</span>
          <div class="fix-step-copy"><strong>Protect the active edit</strong><p>Once claimed, the policy confirms that the current administrator owns the edit and can work without an accidental overwrite.</p></div>
          <figure><a href="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-02.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open the protected active edit interface at full size"><img src="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-02.png" alt="Firepower Management Center showing that the current administrator has locked the policy and is the only person who can edit it" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>The editor gets certainty while the work is active.</figcaption></figure>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>03</span>
          <div class="fix-step-copy"><strong>Signal status clearly</strong><p>Everyone else sees who holds the lock and understands why the policy is temporarily unavailable for editing.</p></div>
          <figure><a href="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-03.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open the policy lock status interface at full size"><img src="/images/change-management/immediate-fix-03.png" alt="Firepower Management Center telling another administrator that Prady has locked the policy and they cannot edit it" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>No guessing, no mystery overwrite, no awkward detective work.</figcaption></figure>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="fix-verdict"><div><span>What it solved</span><p>Invisible concurrent edits and immediate overwrite risk.</p></div><div><span>What it revealed</span><p>Locks alone could not support review, staged release or accountable multi-team work.</p></div></div>
    </section>

    <section id="change-north-star" class="change-chapter north-star-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">06 / North star</p>
      <h2>From policy locking to a collaborative change-control system.</h2>
      <p class="measure">After shipping the quick fix, I regrouped with product and architecture. The outcome shifted from “stop people colliding” to “help teams move changes through an understandable lifecycle.”</p>
      <div class="north-flow"><article><span>Author</span><strong>Create with ownership and context</strong></article><b>→</b><article><span>Review</span><strong>Inspect, question and approve</strong></article><b>→</b><article><span>Stage</span><strong>Prepare the approved release</strong></article><b>→</b><article><span>Deploy</span><strong>Move with visible accountability</strong></article></div>
      <blockquote>“The right answer was not just to block edits. It was to make collaboration legible.”</blockquote>
    </section>

    <section id="change-system" class="change-chapter system-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">07 / Workflow</p>
      <h2>The final design became a workflow—not a scattered collection of actions.</h2>
      <div class="decision-stack">{systemMoves.map(([number,title,copy])=><article><span>{number}</span><div><strong>{title}</strong><p>{copy}</p></div></article>)}</div>
      <figure class="workflow-board">
        <div class="workflow-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Horizontally scrollable ticket-management workflow">
          <img src="/images/change-management/workflow-01-clean.png" alt="End-to-end ticket-management workflow showing policy authoring, validation, submission, review, rejection, approval, staging and deployment across Kit and Nik" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <figcaption><span>End-to-end workflow</span><p>The ticket moves from creation through authoring, review, staging and deployment.</p></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="change-people" class="change-chapter people-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">08 / Kit & Nik</p>
      <h2>Two roles. One workflow. Different responsibility at every stage.</h2>
      <div class="experience-stories">
        <article class="experience-story kit-experience">
          <header><div><span>01 · Policy editor</span><h3>Kit's experience</h3><p>Kit stays focused on assigned work: understand the queue, inspect the ticket and follow its change history.</p></div></header>
          <ol class="experience-path" aria-label="Kit experience stages"><li>Manage my tickets</li><li>Inspect details & comments</li><li>Review change history</li></ol>
          <figure><div class="experience-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Horizontally scrollable Kit policy-editor experience"><img src="/images/change-management/kit-policy-editor-experience.png" alt="Kit policy-editor journey showing the authoring queue, ticket details and comments, and ticket change history" loading="lazy" /></div><figcaption><span>Kit’s view</span><p>One role, three connected moments, and no need to reconstruct the story from several tools.</p></figcaption></figure>
        </article>
        <article class="experience-story nik-experience">
          <header><div><span>02 · Policy editor & administrator</span><h3>Nick's experience</h3><p>Nick works at a wider altitude: coordinate authoring, review submitted work and prepare approved changes for deployment.</p></div></header>
          <ol class="experience-path" aria-label="Nik experience stages"><li>See organisational work</li><li>Filter by status</li><li>Review submissions</li><li>Inspect ticket detail</li><li>Stage approved changes</li></ol>
          <figure><div class="experience-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Horizontally scrollable Nik policy-editor and administrator experience"><img src="/images/change-management/nik-policy-editor-admin-experience.png" alt="Nik administrator journey showing authoring, ticket filtering, review, ticket details and staging approved changes" loading="lazy" /></div><figcaption><span>Nik’s view</span><p>The same system reveals broader control only where the administrator’s responsibility requires it.</p></figcaption></figure>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="people-note">Same product. Different altitude. Nobody gets the “show me everything and hope I find my job” edition.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="change-outcome" class="change-chapter outcome-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">09 / Outcome</p>
      <h2>The work reframed change management as a collaboration problem—not a UI problem.</h2>
      <div class="before-after"><article><span>Immediate value</span><h3>Safer editing now.</h3><ul><li>Visible temporary ownership</li><li>Read-only protection</li><li>Fewer accidental collisions</li></ul></article><article><span>Strategic value</span><h3>A clearer system next.</h3><ul><li>Durable ticket ownership</li><li>Review and staging workflow</li><li>Traceable decisions and handoffs</li></ul></article></div>
      <p class="outcome-note">The leadership value was sequencing: protect the present without allowing the emergency fix to become the permanent product strategy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="change-reflection" class="change-chapter reflection-chapter">
      <p class="change-index">10 / Reflection</p>
      <h2>Good enterprise collaboration is a product model—not a comments panel.</h2>
      <div class="reflection-grid"><article><span>I learned</span><h3>Constraint can sharpen the sequence.</h3><p>The delivery pressure made the immediate risk unmistakable and forced a clean separation between relief and transformation.</p></article><article><span>I wish</span><h3>The system story had started earlier.</h3><p>Pairing the quick fix with the north-star narrative sooner would have helped stakeholders understand why locking was necessary but insufficient.</p></article><article><span>I wonder</span><h3>How far can ownership become proactive?</h3><p>Could future systems detect likely collisions, identify the right reviewer and surface organisational dependencies before work begins?</p></article></div>
    </section>
  </main>
</div>

<style>
